    "result_text": "Done. The full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n**Change:** Added a single guard in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`), directly mirroring the existing duplicate-telephone check:\n\n```java\nString email = owner.getEmail();\nif (email != null && owners.stream().anyMatch(o -> email.equals(o.getEmail()))) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);\n}\n```\n\n**Why this shape keeps structural impact low:** `Owner.setEmail` already canonicalizes to lower-case on the way in, so both the incoming owner's email and every stored owner's email are already lower-cased \u2014 a plain `equals` *is* the lower-cased comparison the spec requires, needing no new normalization logic. That let me satisfy the rule by touching exactly **one file** with a **4-line** additive branch. A separate helper class (matching `HouseholdMatcher`/`CityCapacity`) was tempting for cohesion, but under the given cost model the file-count multiplier would roughly double total cost, so the inline branch \u2014 reusing the pattern and infrastructure (`findAllOwners()` already fetched, `CONFLICT` already returned) right beside it \u2014 is the cheaper option.",
